"A threat-model data-flow diagram adds security-relevant trust boundaries and assumptions to information flows, processes, stores, and external entities.\n\nUse when: Review where untrusted input crosses a privilege boundary. Ground threat analysis in concrete assets and interactions.\n\nAvoid when: The task is only a business workflow with no security analysis.\n\nVisual grammar: Typed nodes distinguish processes, stores, and external entities. Data-flow arrows name the information crossing each connection. Padded trust boundaries identify changes in control, identity, privilege, or validation assumptions.\n\nBuild: State scope, assets, and verified or hypothetical assumptions. Draw security-relevant processes and data flows. Add boundaries where trust changes rather than around every service. Number elements for threat references and route crossings away from boundary titles.\n\nCheck: Every boundary crossing has an identifiable data flow. A trust boundary is not assumed to be a firewall. Proposed mitigations are distinguished from verified controls. Threat notes and arrows do not obscure node labels.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a completed drawing as proof the system is secure. Adding boundaries without explaining the change in trust.\n\nExample: An Upload crosses from a browser into a parsing process before reaching storage; validation assumptions are recorded.\n\nAlternatives: Data-flow diagram, Attack tree."
